 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ for precomputation where $N$ is the number of fine cells. Ray marching time is reduced by a factor proportional to the fraction of skipped macrocells.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$ where $M$ is the number of macrocells.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Precomputation is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of fine cells [S1]. Ray marching time is reduced proportionally to the fraction of skipped macrocells [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$ where $M$ is the number of macrocells [S2].
 *   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   **MRI Dataset:** Increasing skipped macrocells from 0 to 1,190 reduces rendering time from 44.83 ms to 26.05 ms [S1].
-    *   **Heptane Dataset:** Improves time from 51.92 ms to 46.3 ms [S1].
-    *   **Advection Dataset:** Improves time from 58.14 ms to 46.29 ms [S1].
-    *   **Engine Dataset:** Only 12 macrocells have zero density, leading to worse performance (43.61 ms with macrocell vs 41.46 ms without) [S1].
-    *   **Agulhas Dataset:** Despite 1,395,619 skipped macrocells, performance is worse (55.34 ms with macrocell vs 41.53 ms without) due to overhead [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, effective for sparse volumes with large empty regions.
-*   **Limitations:** Overhead can dominate if skipping fraction is low. Performance is transfer-function dependent.
-*   **When to Use:** Sparse volumes with significant empty space.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Dense volumes or when the transfer function results in few zero-density macrocells.
